<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euboea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Evia, Negroponte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euboea is the second-largest Greek island, lying along the east coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and separated from the mainland by the narrow Euripus strait. Its cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and Eretria led early Greek colonisation in the 8th century BCE, founding settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. Under the Venetians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lords it was known as Negroponte, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1470.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euboea.jpg" alt="Euboea" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euboea_from_space.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
